## Bug: `Interval.fromISO` doesn't guard `DateTime.fromISO` against `Settings.throwOnInvalid`

**File / lines:** `/luxon/src/interval.js`, `static fromISO` at lines 134–157; the defect is at **lines 137–138**.

### Current (buggy) code — lines 134–157
```js
134  static fromISO(text, opts) {
135    const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
136    if (s && e) {
137      const start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts),
138        end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
139
140      if (start.isValid && end.isValid) {
141        return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
142      }
143
144      if (start.isValid) {
145        const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
146        if (dur.isValid) {
147          return Interval.after(start, dur);
148        }
149      } else if (end.isValid) {
150        const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
151        if (dur.isValid) {
152          return Interval.before(end, dur);
153        }
154      }
155    }
156    return Interval.invalid("unparsable", `the input "${text}" can't be parsed asISO 8601`);
157  }
```

### Why it's a bug
`DateTime.invalid()` (src/datetime.js:853-865) **throws `InvalidDateTimeError`** when `Settings.throwOnInvalid` is true instead of returning an invalid DateTime. So at line 137/138, parsing the duration half of a valid ISO interval (e.g. `"P1Y"`) throws immediately, before the code can fall back to `Duration.fromISO` at lines 145/150. Result: perfectly valid intervals throw.

Reproduced (unmodified repo, via `npx babel-node`):
```
--- throwOnInvalid = true ---
Interval.fromISO("2016-01-01/P1Y") => THREW InvalidDateTimeError: Invalid DateTime: unparsable: the input "P1Y" ...
Interval.fromISO("P1Y/2016-01-01") => THREW InvalidDateTimeError: Invalid DateTime: unparsable: the input "P1Y" ...
Interval.fromISO("foo/bar")        => THREW InvalidDateTimeError   (should be InvalidIntervalError)
```
With `throwOnInvalid = false` these all behave correctly (the two duration forms return valid intervals, `"foo/bar"` returns `inval

